  "tips": {
    "greetings": "💡 \"Γεια σας\" (YAH-sas) is formal/plural; \"Γεια σου\" (YAH-soo) for friends. Greek people are very friendly — a smile goes a long way.",
    "food": "💡 Ask \"Τι συνιστάτε;\" (What do you recommend?) — Greeks love sharing food culture. Never rush a meal.",
    "directions": "💡 Athens metro is modern and cheap. Greek islands require ferries — book ahead in summer.",
    "numbers": "💡 Currency is Euro (€). Credit cards are now widely accepted, but carry cash for small islands.",
    "emergency": "💡 Dial 112 (EU emergency), 100 (police), 166 (ambulance). Tourist police number: 1571.",
    "shopping": "Cash still matters on the islands. Say yassas when you walk in.",
    "medical": "Call 166 for an ambulance, or 112.",
    "immigration": "Schengen rules apply. A ferry may ask for your passport.",
    "hotel": "Ask whether breakfast and the room tax are included.",
    "transport": "Summer ferries sell out. In Athens, buy a metro ticket before you board."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "Greek Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Greek letters look different but sounds are often familiar — Γ (gamma) before E/I makes a \"y\" sound",
      "The combination ΜΠ (mp) makes a \"b\" sound, and ΝΤ (nt) makes a \"d\" sound at the start of a word",
      "Double vowels αι = \"e\", οι = \"ee\", ου = \"oo\", ει = \"ee\"",
      "The letter Χ (chi) is like the Scottish \"loch\" — a breathy H sound at the back of the throat"
    ]
  },
